<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The Mac App Store sandbox, and nothing speculative.

	  This is the entitlement set a submittable build needs. It is committed as a
	  real file rather than generated inside a recipe so the differences between
	  the two distribution routes stay reviewable: `make build` signs WITHOUT
	  these keys (Developer ID, unnotarized-sandbox, the GitHub/dmg route), and
	  `make deploy-sandbox` signs WITH them, which is the only way to find out
	  what the App Store build can actually do before paying a review cycle for
	  the answer.

	  Read docs/appstore/app-store-metadata.md §2 for the finding this exists to
	  reproduce: the sandbox is not a formality here, it decides which cleanup
	  features can ship at all.
	-->

	<!-- Required for every Mac App Store app. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The user picks the folder or volume to scan. Needed by the picker
	     (`NSOpenPanel`), which is the only sanctioned way to reach a folder
	     outside this app's sandbox.

	     This comment used to claim the scan itself did not need it because
	     "Full Disk Access remains available to a sandboxed app". That is false
	     here, and measuring it is what this file's grant exists to replace:
	     with the SAME bundle identifier and signing identity, so the same TCC
	     grant applied to both, an unsandboxed build reported
	     `FDA.isActive() == true` and read ~/Library/Messages, while a sandboxed
	     build reported false and was denied. tccd logged the grant
	     (`Modify kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAllFiles` for this bundle), so the
	     denial is App Sandbox's own. Full Disk Access does not lift the
	     sandbox, which is why the app neither asks for it when sandboxed nor
	     depends on it for cleanup. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Keep the access the user's folder choice granted, across launches.

	  This is what makes a whole-disk scan possible in the sandbox. No
	  entitlement can grant `/Users`, `/private/var` or `/opt`, and Full Disk
	  Access does not lift App Sandbox — measured with the same bundle
	  identifier and signing identity, the unsandboxed build read
	  `~/Library/Messages` while the sandboxed one was denied, with tccd having
	  logged the grant. So the user chooses the folder once and the app stores a
	  security-scoped bookmark, which is Apple's documented route for exactly
	  this ("Persist file access with security-scoped URL bookmarks").

	  Required, not optional: measured, creating the bookmark without this key
	  fails outright with error 256. An app-scoped bookmark is also bound to the
	  creating bundle identifier — one created by another bundle id failed with
	  error 259 — so each install grants its own folder.

	  Measured effect of resolving one: with no other grant, `~`, `~/Documents`,
	  `~/Downloads` and `~/Projects` went from all DENIED to all readable once
	  `startAccessingSecurityScopedResource()` was called, and back to denied
	  after `stopAccessingSecurityScopedResource()`. The extension is
	  process-wide and must be held only while a scan runs (see
	  `app/ScopedAccess.swift`).
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  RETIRED (2026-10-08): com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.home-relative-path.read-write.

	  This granted the app's own cache folders (`~/.npm`, `~/Library/Caches`,
	  …) so that a Home scan and cleaning worked with no user action. It is
	  gone, for two reasons that were both measured rather than assumed:

	  1. The bookmark route below replaces it. One folder the user picks —
	     `Macintosh HD` — grants the home *and* the rest of the disk, and it is
	     Apple's documented, sanctioned route rather than a temporary exception
	     that has to be justified item by item in review.
	  2. It never covered what the product needs. Measured, with this exception
	     the app still could not read `/System/Volumes/Data/Users`,
	     `/System/Volumes/Data/private/var` or `/opt`, so a whole-disk scan was
	     blind to most of the volume and reported the remainder as the whole.

	  What replaced it, measured on a build with only the sandbox, picker and
	  bookmark entitlements: `/Applications`, `/System/Volumes/Data/Library` and
	  `/Volumes` are readable on the entitlements alone; `/Users` and `/` are
	  not, and are reached through the user's chosen folder. The app therefore
	  asks the user to pick a folder before scanning the disk or the home (see
	  `ScopedAccess` and `ContentView.requestScan`), rather than assuming access
	  it does not have.

	  Retiring this is a review-surface reduction: fewer granted paths, no
	  temporary-exception justification, and one mechanism instead of two.
	-->

	<!-- Outbound HTTP to the model provider the user configured (a local Ollama
	     or LM Studio endpoint, or a hosted one). Without this the custom-provider
	     AI panel cannot connect at all. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The Rust scan engine is linked as a staticlib built outside Xcode, so the
	     binary loads code that is not signed by the app's own team. Required, not
	     optional: without it the app is killed at launch. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  DELIBERATELY ABSENT: com.apple.security.files.user-selected.executable.

	  That entitlement is the only proposed route to launching an external CLI
	  from a sandboxed app, and it was measured (2026-10-06): adding the key alone
	  changes NOTHING, because it only takes effect once the user grants it
	  through a file picker. It therefore cannot express "run whatever is on the
	  user's PATH", which is what the allowlisted cleanup commands do, so it does
	  not rescue that path and is not worth requesting. Adding it here would
	  imply it helps.

	  What the sandbox actually does to the app, measured with the shipping code
	  under this exact entitlement set: the user's login shell still runs, but it
	  sees a container filesystem, so the cleanup tools (`brew`, `npm`, `uv`) it
	  resolves through PATH are not found and exec of an absolute path fails with
	  "doesn't exist". `xcrun` refuses outright ("cannot be used within an App
	  Sandbox"). Those rows do not fail loudly — they find nothing.

	  Use `make deploy-sandbox` to reproduce that, and `make deploy` to go back.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
